WEBVTT

NOTE Cue timings are estimated from sentence length and pauses in the audio.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.776
Welcome to the Concilium Security Risk Brief for Saturday, November twenty eighth.

2
00:00:04.776 --> 00:00:14.006
Five minutes of security coverage from the last twenty four hours, to increase your situational awareness without decreasing your productivity.

3
00:00:14.006 --> 00:00:14.845
Here's what matters today.

4
00:00:14.845 --> 00:00:21.684
Today: a deadly explosion beside a school in eastern Afghanistan, a reported breach of a U.S.

5
00:00:21.684 --> 00:00:32.281
Air Force cloud platform, U.S. forces disabling a blockade runner in the Gulf of Oman, violent protests in Albania's capital, and a foiled truck bomb plot in Colombia.

6
00:00:32.281 --> 00:00:33.223
We start in Afghanistan.

7
00:00:33.223 --> 00:00:44.069
Ariana News reports the Taliban interior ministry spokesman said ten people were killed and one hundred ten injured in an explosion near a school in Asadabad, the capital of Kunar province, this morning.

8
00:00:44.069 --> 00:00:50.069
Local officials earlier put the toll at seven students killed and ninety seven injured.

9
00:00:50.069 --> 00:01:02.321
Al Jazeera, citing a medical official, says the blast happened at a house next to the school, and Global Decipher reports Kunar's information director said explosives stored there for mining work caused the blast.

10
00:01:02.321 --> 00:01:07.706
On cyber, Reuters reports, citing three people familiar with the matter, that hackers breached Cloud One, the U.S.

11
00:01:07.706 --> 00:01:17.038
Air Force's flagship cloud computing platform, potentially exposing sensitive tactical, personnel and logistics data.

12
00:01:17.038 --> 00:01:17.947
Turning to the Gulf.

13
00:01:17.947 --> 00:01:20.749
The Institute for the Study of War says U.S.

14
00:01:20.749 --> 00:01:32.702
Central Command stated that a U.S. aircraft disabled the Palau-flagged bulk carrier Pacific Archer in the Gulf of Oman on Thursday, November twenty sixth, after it left Bandar Abbas and ignored repeated U.S.

15
00:01:32.702 --> 00:01:33.951
Navy warnings to stop.

16
00:01:33.951 --> 00:01:41.600
I S W says it is the first time Central Command has announced disabling a vessel trying to run the blockade on Iranian ports.

17
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:50.920
In an update on Kuwait, I S W says the Kuwaiti and Italian governments confirmed that shrapnel from intercepted Iranian drones killed the two Italian technicians at an air defense site.

18
00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:54.651
Iran denies responsibility.

19
00:01:54.651 --> 00:01:55.343
Next, Albania.

20
00:01:55.343 --> 00:02:05.742
The State Department's travel service says demonstrations continue in central Tirana and around government buildings, and some have turned into violent riots with incendiary devices and thrown rocks.

21
00:02:05.742 --> 00:02:07.359
The U.S.

22
00:02:07.359 --> 00:02:12.182
Embassy in Tirana has advised its personnel to avoid all demonstration areas.

23
00:02:12.182 --> 00:02:23.526
And in Colombia, Noticias Caracol reports the army and police intercepted a truck carrying twelve explosive cylinders and launch ramps on the road to Puerto Gaitán, in Meta department, and captured two people.

24
00:02:23.526 --> 00:02:31.561
Noticias Caracol also reports an explosive attack in Los Patios, Norte de Santander, wounded four police officers.

25
00:02:31.561 --> 00:02:38.393
The Tracking Terrorism and Counter Extremism group attributes the Los Patios attack to suspected E L N militants.

26
00:02:38.393 --> 00:02:41.578
Now a few quick notes by region.

27
00:02:41.578 --> 00:02:53.963
In Africa, an update on Congo's Ebola outbreak: the World Health Organization says it still constitutes a public health emergency of international concern, with more than twelve thousand cases and fifty nine hundred deaths reported.

28
00:02:53.963 --> 00:03:06.653
B B C Africa reports Uganda plans to withdraw its troops from Congo within two weeks as tensions rise between the two countries, and that Guinea-Bissau's military rulers postponed next week's elections by two years.

29
00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:16.438
In Somalia, Baidoa Online reports at least two Puntland soldiers, including a commander, were killed in al Shabaab bomb attacks in the mountains outside Bosaso.

30
00:03:16.438 --> 00:03:23.154
In Europe, Reuters reports Russia says it downed one thousand two hundred forty six Ukrainian drones overnight.

31
00:03:23.154 --> 00:03:37.008
Reuters, citing the Financial Times, reports the U.S. military repelled drones at a U.K. airbase this month, and cites the International Institute for Strategic Studies as saying Russian sabotage attacks in Europe rose sixteen percent.

32
00:03:37.008 --> 00:03:43.548
Reuters also reports, citing three sources, that a Russian convoy carrying military vehicles to Libya turned around after the warship sinking at Benghazi.

33
00:03:43.548 --> 00:03:50.655
Radio Free Europe reports Ukraine has proposed a cease-fire in the Black Sea to allow food exports.

34
00:03:50.655 --> 00:03:59.706
In the Middle East, the Jerusalem Post reports Israeli police and the Shin Bet arrested a suspect in Ramat Hasharon who was thought to be planning a terror attack.

35
00:03:59.706 --> 00:04:10.218
In Asia, Samaa TV reports the M Q M Pakistan party cancelled its planned rally and public gathering in Karachi set for tomorrow.

36
00:04:10.218 --> 00:04:21.355
In Latin America, ACLED says Ecuador holds elections tomorrow with more than fifty one thousand candidates, after armed men killed two candidates at a rally in Huaquillas on November ninth.

37
00:04:21.355 --> 00:04:40.526
On cyber, The Record reports the news agency A F P was hit by a cyberattack on Thursday that forced one of its main distribution platforms offline, that S A P confirmed a cybersecurity incident involving its software development infrastructure, and that Japan's education ministry disclosed a breach of its procurement system.

38
00:04:40.526 --> 00:04:41.944
That's today's brief.

39
00:04:41.944 --> 00:04:50.061
These security developments come from government travel advisories and trusted analysts, curated by the Concilium Security Risk Management Team.

40
00:04:50.061 --> 00:04:58.342
The brief summarizes public reporting and isn't a substitute for your own risk assessment, so check the original source before making travel decisions.

41
00:04:58.342 --> 00:05:00.435
This brief is digitally narrated.

42
00:05:00.435 --> 00:05:01.378
Questions?

43
00:05:01.378 --> 00:05:04.759
Reach Concilium at info at concilium dot U.S.

44
00:05:04.759 --> 00:05:06.758
Thanks for listening, and stay safe.
